    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d527">

                <pb facs="1136" n="1040" xml:id="pg1040"/>

                <head>No. 493.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Evarts to Mr. Baker.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-02-24"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 24,
                        1879</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 35.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch No. 85, of the 24th ultimo,
                    with a copy of the correspondence between yourself and the minister of foreign
                    affairs of Venezuela, in relation to the resolution of the executive that
                    cargoes destined for Puerto Cabello be discharged at La Guayra, has been
                    received.</p>
                <p>In reply I have to state that your non-acquiescence in the order referred to is
                    approved on the ground that Puerto Cabello was in possession of a force hostile
                    to the government, and consequently was not by such order closed to commerce.
                    Such cases are numerous in the relations of this government with the
                    Spanish-American States.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WILLIAM M. EVARTS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
